<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henrician Articles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Henrician Articles were a set of constitutional principles adopted in 1573 and sworn by each newly elected king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, named after the first elected king, Henry of Valois. They guaranteed free royal election, regular sessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, religious toleration and the nobility's right to resist a king who broke them. They were a cornerstone of the Commonwealth's governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henrician-articles.jpg" alt="Henrician Articles" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania_from_the_Statute_(1614).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
